## HEAVY (other seats and gates share this machine and ONE lock)
A heavy job = `dotnet restore`, `dotnet build`, `dotnet test`, `dotnet publish`, a run of a test executable, and a loop of one. **Every heavy job goes through `<lane>/gate/heavy.py`, in the foreground, ONE at a time; you never start `dotnet` or a test executable any other way** (`mutants_c7f.py run` calls your `heavy.py` for each of its jobs; `loop_c7.py` runs only as the ONE command of a `heavy.py` job). A job is `python3 -I '<lane>/gate/heavy.py' '<lane>' 'B1961 seat X44' <label> '<clone>' [K=V ...] -- dotnet <arguments>` (or a test executable's absolute path and `-noColor`, or `/usr/bin/python3 -I '<lane>/gate/loop_c7.py' ...`), a new label for every job; `CI=true` and `TIMEOUT=<s>` go before the --. The runner reads load1 FIRST and waits while it is 7 or more (at most 30 minutes, then `BLOCKED: load`), THEN takes the wave's lock by `mkdir` of EXACTLY `/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/!CODING/Datasec/OXPd_2/.tools/wave1-heavy.lock.d` with `owner.txt`, runs the ONE command under a time bound (900 s by default), and releases the lock by MOVING the folder to `<lane>/_quarantine/locks/`. **Gate B1960 (lane A2-F2) and Tuesday's gates and drafters share this lock: an `owner.txt` is never yours to break. Allow 30 minutes of waits per job and about 40 in all**; `BLOCKED: lock held <its owner line>` after that is a relaunch for Tuesday, not a finding. The drafter ran this runner unchanged for every job (MEASURED: lock waits 0 to 6; a build 2 to 36 s, the whole solution's test 9 to 14 s, the Contracts executable about 6 s red and about 2 to 5 s green). Never hold the lock while you read, edit or wait on a hosted run. **Shell form:** ONE simple command per call; no `$( ... )` inside a loop, no long `&&` chains; multi-step logic goes into a `python3 -I` script under `<lane>/scratch/` that you then run. zsh does not split an unquoted variable: write each command out in full or wrap it in `bash -c`.

